1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,295
Computer science, the major built for the tech economy,

2
00:00:03,295 --> 00:00:05,932
is now one of the majors most exposed to AI.

3
00:00:05,932 --> 00:00:08,448
Nursing and education are among the least.

4
00:00:08,568 --> 00:00:10,244
And it's already showing up.

5
00:00:10,244 --> 00:00:13,596
In Texas, the more of a major's tasks AI could automate,

6
00:00:13,596 --> 00:00:17,187
the fewer of its graduates had jobs a year after graduating.

7
00:00:17,307 --> 00:00:20,908
Forty-two percent of students working toward a bachelor's degree

8
00:00:20,908 --> 00:00:24,283
have seriously thought about switching majors because of AI.

9
00:00:24,403 --> 00:00:27,027
So which majors are actually safe from AI?

10
00:00:30,344 --> 00:00:31,626
No major is AI-proof.

11
00:00:31,626 --> 00:00:33,520
Nobody's data can promise that.

12
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:34,375
But this year,

13
00:00:34,375 --> 00:00:37,306
research has started to show where AI is already

14
00:00:37,306 --> 00:00:40,176
showing up in the job market for new graduates.

15
00:00:40,176 --> 00:00:42,192
And it points somewhere specific.

16
00:00:42,192 --> 00:00:43,230
Not mass layoffs.

17
00:00:43,230 --> 00:00:44,146
The front door.

18
00:00:44,496 --> 00:00:46,503
Start with that Texas study.

19
00:00:46,503 --> 00:00:51,665
The Dallas Fed followed graduates of Texas public universities by major,

20
00:00:51,665 --> 00:00:52,955
from 2021 to 2024.

21
00:00:52,955 --> 00:00:57,830
Most exposed: computer science, computer engineering, and languages.

22
00:00:57,830 --> 00:01:01,414
Least exposed: nursing, education, and psychology.

23
00:01:01,534 --> 00:01:05,372
For every ten points more of a major's tasks that AI could automate,

24
00:01:05,372 --> 00:01:07,742
the share of its graduates employed a year

25
00:01:07,742 --> 00:01:10,168
out was about one point seven points lower.

26
00:01:10,168 --> 00:01:14,401
And first-year earnings in the more exposed majors fell about five percent,

27
00:01:14,401 --> 00:01:15,699
relative to the others.

28
00:01:15,819 --> 00:01:16,454
Nationally,

29
00:01:16,454 --> 00:01:20,323
the Cleveland Fed found unemployment has risen about one percentage

30
00:01:20,323 --> 00:01:24,250
point more in the jobs most exposed to AI than in the least exposed.

31
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:28,235
For workers aged twenty-three to thirty, the gap is about two points.

32
00:01:28,355 --> 00:01:33,310
Anthropic's own usage data shows a smaller version of the same thing: workers aged

33
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:38,265
twenty-two to twenty-five are getting into AI-exposed jobs less often than before.

34
00:01:38,265 --> 00:01:41,347
For workers over twenty-five, there's no such drop.

35
00:01:41,467 --> 00:01:46,332
And Stanford payroll data shows employment in the jobs most exposed

36
00:01:46,332 --> 00:01:50,616
to AI fell about eleven percent from late 2022 to mid-2026,

37
00:01:50,616 --> 00:01:54,246
while in the least exposed jobs it rose about ten.

38
00:01:54,366 --> 00:01:58,312
So far, AI is changing who gets in, more than who gets pushed out.

39
00:01:58,662 --> 00:02:04,186
So if you only look at who's getting hired, the safest majors look like education.

40
00:02:04,186 --> 00:02:06,341
In the New York Fed's 2024 data,

41
00:02:06,341 --> 00:02:10,787
special education grads had zero point seven percent unemployment.

42
00:02:10,787 --> 00:02:13,212
Elementary education, one point two.

43
00:02:13,332 --> 00:02:14,569
But look at the pay.

44
00:02:14,569 --> 00:02:18,526
Special education grads start around forty-six thousand dollars.

45
00:02:18,526 --> 00:02:20,566
Elementary education, forty-five.

46
00:02:20,566 --> 00:02:24,894
The typical starting salary across all majors is fifty-eight thousand.

47
00:02:25,014 --> 00:02:26,141
Safe by one measure.

48
00:02:26,141 --> 00:02:28,001
Among the lowest paid by another.

49
00:02:28,351 --> 00:02:31,204
Microsoft researchers measured how much of each

50
00:02:31,204 --> 00:02:33,996
job's work AI chatbots can actually help with.

51
00:02:33,996 --> 00:02:38,124
At the top: interpreters and translators, writers, customer service.

52
00:02:38,124 --> 00:02:42,555
At the bottom: phlebotomists, oral surgeons, hazardous materials removal.

53
00:02:42,555 --> 00:02:45,226
Work done with hands, on a body, in a place.

54
00:02:45,346 --> 00:02:48,770
They also say a high score doesn't mean a job disappears.

55
00:02:48,770 --> 00:02:51,354
It means AI can help with more of the work.

56
00:02:51,354 --> 00:02:53,276
And it isn't just low-paid work.

57
00:02:53,276 --> 00:02:53,997
Across jobs,

58
00:02:53,997 --> 00:02:58,743
they found only a weak link between how much AI can help and what the job pays.

59
00:02:58,863 --> 00:03:01,934
And healthcare, where so much of the work is hands-on,

60
00:03:01,934 --> 00:03:04,152
is where the government expects growth.

61
00:03:04,152 --> 00:03:07,678
The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects U.S. jobs overall will

62
00:03:07,678 --> 00:03:11,090
grow about three and a half percent over the next ten years.

63
00:03:11,090 --> 00:03:13,364
Healthcare practitioners, eight percent.

64
00:03:13,364 --> 00:03:16,378
Office and administrative support, down four percent,

65
00:03:16,378 --> 00:03:19,620
and the Bureau names AI-powered automation as one reason.

66
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:24,394
The fastest-growing job in the country: nurse practitioner, up forty-one percent,

67
00:03:24,394 --> 00:03:28,359
with a median pay of about a hundred and thirty-two thousand dollars.

68
00:03:28,709 --> 00:03:32,171
So the useful list is the majors that do well on both counts.

69
00:03:32,171 --> 00:03:35,179
In the New York Fed data: below-average unemployment,

70
00:03:35,179 --> 00:03:37,733
and starting pay of seventy thousand or more.

71
00:03:37,853 --> 00:03:41,995
Nursing: two point one percent unemployed, seventy thousand to start.

72
00:03:41,995 --> 00:03:46,017
And only about thirteen percent of nursing grads are underemployed,

73
00:03:46,017 --> 00:03:48,178
against thirty-nine percent overall.

74
00:03:48,298 --> 00:03:52,167
Civil engineering: two point three percent, seventy-five thousand.

75
00:03:52,167 --> 00:03:56,096
Aerospace engineering: two point two percent, eighty-five thousand.

76
00:03:56,096 --> 00:04:00,082
Construction services: two point two percent, seventy-five thousand.

77
00:04:00,082 --> 00:04:03,893
Electrical engineering, finance, and economics make the list too.

78
00:04:04,013 --> 00:04:06,674
And the jobs these majors often lead to are

79
00:04:06,674 --> 00:04:09,273
projected to grow over the next ten years.

80
00:04:09,273 --> 00:04:11,191
Registered nurses, six percent.

81
00:04:11,191 --> 00:04:12,985
Civil engineers, six percent.

82
00:04:12,985 --> 00:04:14,594
Financial analysts, seven.

83
00:04:14,944 --> 00:04:17,853
Many of these jobs share something else: a license.

84
00:04:17,853 --> 00:04:21,104
Seventy-one percent of healthcare practitioners hold one.

85
00:04:21,104 --> 00:04:23,328
In computer and math jobs, six percent.

86
00:04:23,448 --> 00:04:27,197
A civil engineer needs a license to sign off on public projects.

87
00:04:27,197 --> 00:04:29,188
An accountant needs a CPA license,

88
00:04:29,188 --> 00:04:32,527
which takes a hundred and fifty semester hours of school.

89
00:04:32,527 --> 00:04:35,104
AI can help draft the plans and the numbers.

90
00:04:35,104 --> 00:04:36,803
Someone licensed still signs.

91
00:04:36,803 --> 00:04:40,024
That's our reading, not something these tables measure.

92
00:04:40,144 --> 00:04:42,778
And not every safe path is a four-year degree.

93
00:04:42,778 --> 00:04:45,527
Electricians are projected to grow nine percent,

94
00:04:45,527 --> 00:04:49,936
after a four- to five-year apprenticeship, and most states require a license.

95
00:04:50,286 --> 00:04:51,501
Now computer science.

96
00:04:51,501 --> 00:04:55,897
Higher pay to start, at eighty-seven thousand, but seven percent unemployed.

97
00:04:56,017 --> 00:04:57,389
But tech isn't one job.

98
00:04:57,389 --> 00:05:00,970
Software developers are still projected to grow ten percent,

99
00:05:00,970 --> 00:05:04,729
at a median of about a hundred and thirty-six thousand dollars.

100
00:05:04,729 --> 00:05:07,713
Computer programmers, the more routine coding job,

101
00:05:07,713 --> 00:05:12,188
are projected to shrink seven percent, and the Bureau names AI as a reason.

102
00:05:12,308 --> 00:05:13,474
In Anthropic's data,

103
00:05:13,474 --> 00:05:16,213
AI is already being used for about seventy-five

104
00:05:16,213 --> 00:05:18,602
percent of a computer programmer's tasks.

105
00:05:18,602 --> 00:05:21,400
For about thirty percent of workers, like cooks,

106
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:24,489
mechanics, and bartenders, it hasn't shown up at all.

107
00:05:24,609 --> 00:05:25,749
It's not only tech.

108
00:05:25,749 --> 00:05:28,989
Graphic designers are projected to shrink two percent,

109
00:05:28,989 --> 00:05:31,329
with AI design tools named as a reason.

110
00:05:31,449 --> 00:05:33,404
So the question isn't only the major.

111
00:05:33,404 --> 00:05:34,777
It's which work inside it.

112
00:05:35,127 --> 00:05:37,053
These are patterns, not proof.

113
00:05:37,053 --> 00:05:40,457
The exposure measures don't use the same definitions.

114
00:05:40,457 --> 00:05:43,282
Most of this data is about jobs, not majors.

115
00:05:43,282 --> 00:05:47,970
The major figures are one year, 2024, with small samples for some majors.

116
00:05:48,090 --> 00:05:49,631
And hiring hasn't collapsed.

117
00:05:49,631 --> 00:05:52,769
Employers surveyed this spring planned to hire about five

118
00:05:52,769 --> 00:05:56,016
and a half percent more new graduates than the year before.

119
00:05:56,016 --> 00:06:00,420
Just over a quarter of them said AI has cut the need for some entry-level tasks.

120
00:06:00,770 --> 00:06:02,545
So, which majors are AI-proof?

121
00:06:02,545 --> 00:06:03,433
None, honestly.

122
00:06:03,433 --> 00:06:06,096
But some majors are doing well on both counts

123
00:06:06,096 --> 00:06:08,640
that matter for a first job: getting hired,

124
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:09,646
and getting paid.

125
00:06:09,646 --> 00:06:13,848
And many of them lead to work tied to a place, a license, or a patient.

126
00:06:13,968 --> 00:06:16,916
A better question than "is it AI-proof?" is this:

127
00:06:16,916 --> 00:06:19,803
does this major lead to work that AI helps with,

128
00:06:19,803 --> 00:06:21,067
or work that AI does?

129
00:06:21,067 --> 00:06:22,330
And what does it pay?
